          %h2#overview Overview
          %p
            Matching
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/asymptotic+series"} asymptotic series
            expansions of certain functions like
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Airy+functions"} Airy functions
            and more generally of solutions to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+equations"} wave equations
            (say in short wave approximation or solutions to non-Fuchsian meromorphic ODEs) comes with sharp changes (disconintuities) at certain phase angles (Stokes lines) described by certain factors, Stokes matrices.
          %p
            G. G. Stokes has discovered these phenomena – now also known more generally as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wall+crossing"} wall crossing
            phenomena – in the study of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+optics"} geometric optics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Airy+function"} Airy function
            which is an example of a solution to a linear meromorphic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+differential+equation"} ordinary differential equation
            which has an irregular singular point, hence the equation is not
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fuchsian+equation"} Fuchsian
          %p Stokes factors (multipliers, matrices) appear also in the study of stability in the geometry of moduli spaces of sheaves. According to Toledano Laredo “Joyce’s wall-crossing formulae for invariants counting semistable objects in an abelian category A may be understood as Stokes phenomena for a connection on the Riemann sphere taking value in the Ringel-Hall Lie algebra of A.”.
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Airy+function"} Airy function
            %li
              %p
